AB  - Marine chemoautotrophic bacteria, driven by the microbial carbon pump, play a crucial role in oceanic carbon sequestration through continuous dark carbon fixation (DCF). Using marine DCF for industrial CO2 utilization is our persistent pursuit. However, due to the low density of chemoautotrophic bacteria in the marine system, efficiently amplifying DCF at reactor scale still faces many challenges. This study investigates the bottlenecks and strategies to amplify DCF at reactor scale. It was found that increased exogenous organics in seawater significantly reduce carbon fixation efficiency by decreasing the abundance of cbb genes and inhibiting transcriptional activity. Similarly, endogenous organic metabolites (EOMs) in closed bioreactors showed inhibitory effects, with carbon fixation rates dropping to 47.2 % and 26.3 % of the control reactor under low and high metabolite concentrations, respectively. To address this issue, a perfusion bioreactor was developed, enhancing fixation persistence by 3.7-fold through continuous removal of free organic metabolites and retention of bacterial biomass. Techno-economic analysis suggests that optimized reactors could effectively achieve DCF using industrial waste as nutrients. These findings highlight the significant scalability potential for marine DCF under controlled conditions, offering a promising pathway for industrial carbon fixation technologies.
