AB  - Seagrass meadows rank among the most effective blue-carbon sinks. However, the interactive influences of seasonal and tidal dynamics on their sediment organic carbon (SOC) storage remain inadequately understood. Here, we quantified SOC storage, SOC deposition rates, and plant-sediment carbon coupling across low-to-high tidal gradients in two tropical lagoon-type bays located on Hainan Island in the South China Sea, during the dry (November to May) and rainy (June to October) seasons. Results indicate that SOC density, aboveground plant carbon density, and SOC deposition rate all decline with increasing tidal levels. Seasonality exerts a dominant control on SOC deposition rate in seagrass meadows, as the dry season alone delivered 104.9 g OC m-2 yr- 1, and contributing a striking 95% of the annual total (mean 110 g OC m- 2 yr- 1). Seagrass plant carbon density (aboveand belowground) and sediment nutrient levels (NH4+-N, NO3 --N, IN and silt + clay fraction) explained over 75% of the variance in SOC deposition rates, with only silt + clay content showing a negative correlation, indicating hydrodynamic decoupling over texture control. Structural equation models identified plant carbon input as the primary driver of SOC density, supplemented by SOC deposition rates. Tidal level had dual effects: a direct negative path and a positive indirect path mediated via SOC deposition rates. Our findings demonstrate that intermittent seasonal windows, rather than annual means, govern the majority of carbon sequestration in tropical seagrass meadows, and that tidal-seasonal coupling must be explicitly resolved in blue-carbon assessments rather than as background variability. Prioritizing low tidal zones for seagrass restoration will maximize carbon accrual. The findings highlight that integrating seasonal biomass and deposition dynamics sharpens the precision of carbon sequestration estimates for global blue-carbon ecosystems.
