AB  - Sediment organic carbon (SOC) sequestration is a critical component of mangrove ecosystems. To better understand how mangrove restoration modulates SOC sequestration and the driving factors involved, fieldwork was conducted across six sites in Kaozhou Bay, including the natural native Avicennia marina, the alien Sonneratia apetala, and restored mangroves (mangroves and adjacent mudflats) in two regions. The results revealed a wide range of SOC density in the depth of top meter, ranging from 15.1 to 144.0 Mg C ha(-1), with the highest values in natural A. marina, followed by alien S. apetala and the two restored mangrove ecosystems (P < 0.05). No difference in SOC density was found between restored mangroves and their adjacent mudflats within each region, suggesting that mangrove restoration did not significantly enhance carbon accumulation over a decade. However, the SOC density in both restored regions showed significant differences (P < 0.05) due to cofferdam restoration. The findings of this study will facilitate policy decisions concerning the potential carbon sequestration benefits of mangrove restoration in China.
