AB  - Monitoring gross primary productivity (GPP) of mangroves is crucial for quantifying the carbon sequestration potential of these blue carbon ecosystems (BCEs). Solar-induced fluorescence (SIF) plays an important role in detecting plant photosynthesis. However, research on the relationship between SIF and GPP in mangroves remains limited. We collected high-frequency SIF observations in a restored mangrove ecosystem in Southeast China during 2021-2023 using tower-based remote sensing. We then investigated the SIF-GPP relationship at different temporal scales and the influence of environmental factors on the SIF-GPP relationship using high-frequency SIF observations and high-frequency GPP data. Our results demonstrate that the robust relationship between SIF and GPP is predominantly driven by absorbed photosynthetically active radiation, with the SIF-GPP relationship transitioning from a nonlinear to a linear pattern from the half-hourly to the daily scale. Sensitivity thresholds for air temperature (Ta = 17.01 (degrees) C) and vapor pressure deficit (VPD = 9.59 hPa) were identified, beyond which their effects on the SIF-GPP relationship shifted from inhibition to promotion. The suppressive effect of tidal inundation on the SIF-GPP relationship in mangroves was significantly amplified under high Ta, high VPD, or their combined conditions. Tides weakened the role of canopy structure in explaining the SIF-GPP relationship by suppressing the relationship between light use efficiency (LUE) and escape factor of SIF photons ( f(esc) ). Although the high accuracy of SIF-based GPP estimation models underscores the potential of SIF for tracking GPP, the negative impact of tidal influences on SIF-based GPP estimation cannot be overlooked. These findings highlight the complex interplay between tidal effects and environmental conditions in modulating the SIF-GPP relationship in mangrove ecosystems.
