AB  - Mangrove forests, as a quintessential blue carbon ecosystem, play a vital role in the global carbon cycle and climate regulation. However, studies on the spatial distribution patterns of mangrove carbon stocks, intercommunity variability, and tidal influence remain limited in typical regions of China. In this article, we conducted a systematic field investigation in the Shankou Mangrove National Nature Reserve, Guangxi, based on 40 vegetation sampling plots and 22 Soil coring transects, to comprehensively evaluate the carbon stock characteristics and their spatial heterogeneity within the mangrove ecosystem. The results revealed that the total carbon stock density in the study area was 120.41MgC<middle dot>ha(-11), with biomass carbon accounting for 55.64MgC<middle dot>ha(-11)- lower than the reported national average for Chinese mangroves (84.61 MgC<middle dot>ha(-11))- which is consistent with the characteristics of subtropical mangrove ecosystems. Notably, Soil carbon accounted for 53.77% of the total stock, representing the dominant carbon pool. Spatially, carbon stocks exhibited a clear "high in core zones, low in peripheral zones" pattern. Carbon sequestration capacity varied significantly among plant assemblages and tidal zones, with arboreal communities dominated by Bruguiera gymnorhiza and Rhizophora stylosa demonstrating the highest accumulation potential. Additionally, soil carbon density decreased progressively from the mid-intertidal zone toward the high- and low-intertidal zones, indicating that tidal inundation frequency exerts a crucial control on carbon burial and stabilization. These findings provide a valuable scientific basis and empirical dataset for mangrove carbon accounting, ecosystem restoration, and blue carbon management strategies.
