AB  - The escalating reliance on fossil fuels has increased atmospheric CO2 concentrations, intensifying the global climate challenge and necessitating scalable mitigation strategies. This review critically evaluates carbon capture technologies across carbon capture and storage (CCS), carbon capture, utilisation and storage (CCUS), and carbon dioxide removal (CDR), including emerging pathways such as direct air capture, bioenergy with carbon capture and storage, and ocean alkalinity enhancement. Despite rapid advances in materials and process engineering, existing studies remain fragmented, with limited integration of separation mechanisms, material performance, regeneration strategies, techno-economic feasibility, and scalability within a unified comparative framework. A separation-principle-driven framework is applied to systematically compare absorption, adsorption, membrane, cryogenic, and chemical-looping processes, enabling mechanistic evaluation of energy penalties, regeneration requirements, material durability, and deployment constraints. Advanced solid sorbents demonstrate CO2 uptakes of approximately 3-4 mmol g(-1) under controlled conditions, although limitations persist in moisture tolerance, scalability, and long-term stability. Hybrid regeneration strategies show potential to reduce thermal penalties, whereas conventional amine systems typically require similar to 3-4 GJ t(-1) of CO2 for regeneration. Techno-economic analysis indicates that point-source capture technologies currently cost similar to$50-100 t(-1) CO2, whereas engineered carbon removal approaches remain significantly higher at similar to$200-600 t(-1) CO2. This review introduces a unified analytical framework that integrates separation physics, energetics, material performance, and technology readiness to enable cross-comparison of capture pathways. The analysis identifies scalable deployment opportunities for the 2025-2035 horizon and highlights that energy demand, material durability, and infrastructure integration remain critical barriers to large-scale implementation.
