AB  - Carbon dioxide release from the Southern Ocean is generally recognized as a primary factor driving atmospheric carbon dioxide rise during the deglaciation; however, the B & oslash;lling-Aller & oslash;d period saw an atmospheric carbon dioxide plateau, despite diminished biological pump and enhanced ventilation in the Southern Ocean favoring carbon dioxide outgassing. Here we, by compiling productivity records based on multiple geochemical and paleontological proxies, suggest that this plateau was achieved by basin-wide elevated biological pump in the oligotrophic North Pacific. This basin-scale carbon sink is a result of oligotrophy and stratification, allowing for prolonged and complete consumption of deep-sourced nutrients and carbon dioxide in the deepened euphotic zone when North Pacific Intermediate Water was sluggish. This carbon sink is amplified by synchronously enhanced stratification-induced nitrogen fixation. Extending the B & oslash;lling-Aller & oslash;d warming-biological pump analogy to current and future warming scenarios, however, is constrained by human-forced biogeochemical-physical processes, yielding large uncertainties in biological pump responses and feedbacks.
