AB  - Microalgae is considered an advanced biomass feedstock to provide various high-valued bio-products from different pathways. Biochar from microalgal biomass has been a flagship solid biofuel product that can alleviate the global energy demand. With the heightened demand for energy storage technologies such as battery electrodes, the conversion of microalgal biochar to graphite is a growing field of study. Hence, the sustainable production of graphite-based battery electrodes from microalgal biomass needs to be assessed. This work evaluated the sustainable production of microalgal biochar as a potential graphite alternative for battery electrodes through a comprehensive life cycle assessment (LCA) of 12 scenarios. The scenarios varied in CO2 source, pretreatment methods, and pyrolysis temperature. This novel approach addresses carbon utilization and sustainable material synthesis by integrating CO2 capture from industrial sources with microalgal cultivation. The results showed that biomass treated with two-step torrefaction and pyrolysis at 700 degrees C had the highest carbon content (62.50 wt%), closely followed by 600 degrees C (62.16 wt%), demonstrating its suitability for graphite application. Scenarios utilizing CO2 from flue gas exhibited net negative greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions (-155 to -263 kg CO2-eq), whereas scenarios using CO2 from gas tanks resulted in positive GHG emissions (600-708 kg CO2-eq). Further analysis of categories revealed that human carcinogenic toxicity, freshwater ecotoxicity, marine ecotoxicity, terrestrial ecotoxicity, and freshwater eutrophication as key factors. Scenarios utilizing CO2 from flue gas demonstrated lower impacts across these categories than scenarios using CO2 from gas tanks. Scenario i, employing flue gas CO2 and the two-step process, emerged as the most environmentally favorable (-256.165 kg CO2-eq) while delivering high-quality biochar. These findings highlight microalgal biochar's promise as a sustainable graphite substitute. They also emphasize the need for future research into renewable energy integration, process scale-up, and techno-economic analyses to enhance its feasibility and industrial adoption.
