AB  - Excessive and continuous use of fossil fuels has exacerbated the global greenhouse effect, prompting a persistent search for alternative energy sources and carbon emission reduction. Microalgae are the earliest photoautotrophic planktonic microorganisms on earth, widely distributed in oceans, rivers, lakes and wetlands. They can convert absorbed carbon dioxide or inorganic carbon sources into valuable biosynthetic compounds, such as oils and fats, through photosynthesis. Thus, the cultivation of microalgae for carbon sequestration and targeted lipid production is a promising sustainable energy alternative to replace conventional fossil fuels in the future. This review aims to summarize the last 10 years of research advancements in lipid synthesis and lipid accumulation regulation in microalgae, including the mechanisms of lipid synthesis, regulatory methods of lipid production, and the physiological mechanisms of lipid accumulation in microalgae, so as to provide valuable insights for the exploration of new methods of microalgae lipid production and industrial application of microalgae lipid production in the future.
