AB  - Although both Spartina alterniflora invasion and native vegetation restoration profoundly affect the carbon sequestration function and microbial ecology of coastal wetlands, the regulatory mechanisms underlying their impacts on total organic carbon (TOC) accumulation and microbial community responses remain insufficiently understood. In this study, variations in TOC and microbial community characteristics were systematically investigated using environmental DNA technology in Spartina alterniflora-invaded (SAI) and Scirpus mariqueterrestored (SMR) zones of Hangzhou Bay, China. Gaetbulibacter and Desulfofrigus served as indicator genera in the SAI region, whereas Demequina, Paracoccus, Microbacterium, Agromyces, Labrenzia, and Mesorhizobium were enriched in the SMR region. Despite no significant differences in TOC content between the SAI and SMR, TOC remained a pivotal driver of bacterial diversity in SAI, while its role as an environmental filter was markedly attenuated in SMR. Higher node connectivity and centrality in the SMR region indicate that vegetation restoration fosters tighter microbial interactions, enhancing functional redundancy and ecosystem resilience to disturbance. Compared with SAI, microbial community assembly in SMR was relatively more influenced by stochastic processes, with a reduced effect of TOC on environmental filtering. These findings reveal substantial differences in the microbial ecological processes underlying carbon cycling between SAI and SMR and highlight the inherent limitations of TOC as a blue carbon indicator. Future research should consider integrating microbial community dynamics to better elucidate the mechanisms and response pathways regulating wetland carbon cycling.
